  growth and it can be transformed into  something greater than merely a feeling  so sadness you know if he says it's an  important moment of tension spawn oh and  he describes it this is the way he he  says we feel as paralysis because we no  longer here are astonished emotions  living we are alone with the unfamiliar  presence that has entered us everything  we trust and are used to is for a moment  taken away from us we stand in the midst  of a transition where we cannot remain  standing this actually sounds not only  like sadness and in the sense that we're  used to but also like existential  anxiety as other figures like  Kierkegaard or Heidegger or our Sartre  have described it as well with these  sort of markers of everything being  taken away  Rilke stresses that when sadness occurs  it's not simply because of loss it's not  simply because of frustration or  deprivation it's because something new  has entered into us and we're not quite  sure what to make of it so he says that  there are moments when we we are sad  when something new is entered us  something unknown our feelings grow mute  in shy embarrassment a silence arises  and in the new experience which no one  knows stands in the midst of it all and  says nothing and you can think about the  sadness is that that you feel sometimes  which overcome you they surprise you you  know you could be watching something or  listening to something and suddenly you  feel sad and why is that well something  is happening within you you may not be  able to put your finger on it right away  but he talks about this new presence  within us  he says the presence that has been added  as entered our heart gone into its  internal  chamber and is no longer even there it's  already in our bloodstream it's become  part of ourselves  and we don't know what it was and he  says we have changed there's a beautiful  metaphor like a house that a guest has  entered changes like a house in which a  new presence has come in which alters  the relations between everything else  and Rilke thinks that there's some  mistakes that that people make one of  them he talks about so the only  sadnesses that are actually dangerous  and unhealthy are the ones that we carry  around in public why in order to drown  them out with the noise like diseases  that are treated superficially and  foolishly they withdraw and after a  short interval break out again the more  terribly and gather inside us and our  life life that is unlived rejected lost  life here another beautiful turn of  phrase life that we can die of so there  are way in which our own effective  emotional states can be turned against  us we can't really get rid of sadness by  going out to the bar and having some  drinks with friends not a real sadness  not something that is within us maybe a  superficial feeling blue you can but  going out to the mall and purchasing  something hanging out with your friends  watching endless shows on Netflix  whatever other recourse that you might  have you know hooking up with somebody  and losing yourself in sex the sadness  will always come back if it's there to  begin with it will always reemerge  ladies he says like a disease that has  been treated superficially and foolishly  and then it can become something bad for  us another mistake he talks about he  says that you know one of the things  that that we've we've done and doing a  lot of harm to life is rejecting what is  presenting itself to us he says we must  accept our reality as vastly as we  possibly can  everything even the unprecedented must  be possible within us this is the  courage that's required of us the  courage to face the strangest most  unusual most inexplicable experiences  that can meet us and one of those  experiences for so many of us is sadness  a sadness that we don't understand a  sadness that comes upon us and we're not  sure why it's there we could just simply  reject it or we can take in a different  attitude towards it and so the fact