ns by which  an organism frees itself of something  this is like a sickness you must allow  it to take its course and pay attention  to it rather than trying to drug it up  or find the causes and rip those out and  deal with it in that way now what is  Rilke suggests in place of this taking  on one's own fate one chickasaw in  German the fate that is in fact he says  within you not something imposed on you  from the outside and allowing it its  development its end victim its unfolding  in in the German that he's writing him  here he tells us that this is why it is  so important to be solitary and  attentive to pay attention Alf American  when one is sad why because the  seemingly uneventful and motionless  moment when our future steps into us the  future he says is stepping inside of us  our future is already within us he says  the future steps into us is much closer  to life and that other loud an  accidental point of time when it happens  to us from outside so Rilke is saying  that there's two points in time or  moments or periods of time in which our  future is manifesting itself to us he  says the quieter we are the more deeply  and serenely the new presence can enter  us the more we can make in our own the  more it becomes our fate our shiksa  then later when it happens that it steps  forth out of us to other people it  becomes public we will feel related and  close to it in our innermost being and  that is necessary he says other people  get it wrong they think that fate comes  from the outside but instead it actually  emerges from us he says it's only  because so many people have not absorbed  and transformed their fates that were  living in them that they have not  realized what was emerging from them it  was so alien that in their confusion and  fear they thought it must have entered  them at the very moment they became  aware when it was actually there and he  says the future stands still we move an  infinite space so another possibility  for us is to take